  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 6,
    "lesson_title": "Multiplying a Function by a Shifted Impulse",
    "lesson_core_move": "Convert $f(t)$$\\delta$(t - t0) into $f(t0)$$\\delta$(t - t0) by evaluating the function at the shifted impulse location.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Multiplying a Function by a Shifted Impulse",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Multiplying a Function by a Shifted Impulse",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "If you already know the ordinary impulse rule $f(t)\\delta(t)=f(0)\\delta(t)$, the shifted case uses the same idea. The cue is the factor $\\delta(t-t_0)$, which tells you to sample the surrounding function at the time where the impulse argument is zero.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the action is simple: replace every remaining $t$ in $f(t)$ with $t_0$ and leave the shifted impulse in place. In symbolic work, the impulse localizes the product at $t=t_0$ instead of letting the full function of time survive.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "f(t)\\delta(t-t_0)=f(t_0)\\delta(t-t_0)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "For the base case, a shift of $2$ means the impulse is located at $t=2$, so $f(t)\\delta(t-2)=f(2)\\delta(t-2)$. That is the same substitution rule, just applied at a specific impulse location.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "f(t-a)\\delta(t-a)=f(a)\\delta(t-a)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Any shifted impulse works this way, including negative shifts written as $\\delta(t+a)$. Later sections will use this substitution rule directly to simplify concrete products quickly.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Multiplying a Function by a Shifted Impulse",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Evaluating a Function at the Impulse Location",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Evaluate and simplify $f(t)\\delta(t-5)$ for $f(t)=2t+3$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is the shifted impulse $\\delta(t-5)$, so the multiplier is sampled at $t=5$ while the impulse stays centered at $5$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\nf(t)\\delta(t-5) &= f(5)\\delta(t-5) \\\\\n&= (2(5)+3)\\delta(t-5) \\\\\n&= 13\\delta(t-5)\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the simplified result is $13\\delta(t-5)$. The function has been evaluated at the impulse location, and the shifted impulse argument is unchanged.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Multiplying a Function by a Shifted Impulse",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Multiplying by a Shifted Impulse at a Negative Time",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Evaluate $f(t)\\delta(t+3)$ for $f(t)=t^2-1$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Read $\\delta(t+3)$ as $\\delta(t-(-3))$, so the sample point is $t=-3$, not $t=+3$. The shifted impulse tells you to evaluate the multiplier there and keep the same impulse argument.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\nf(t)\\delta(t+3) &= f(-3)\\delta(t+3) \\\\\n&= \\big((-3)^2-1\\big)\\delta(t+3) \\\\\n&= 8\\delta(t+3)\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the simplified product is $8\\delta(t+3)$. The negative shift changes only the location where the function is sampled.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Multiplying a Function by a Shifted Impulse",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Combining Function Values with Scaled Shifted Impulses",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Simplify $2(4t+2)\\delta(t-4)-3\\delta(t-4)$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Both terms use the same shifted impulse $\\delta(t-4)$, so first evaluate the multiplier $2(4t+2)$ at the impulse location $t=4$. Then keep the common impulse factor and combine the coefficients.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\n2(4t+2)\\delta(t-4)-3\\delta(t-4) &= 2\\big(4(4)+2\\big)\\delta(t-4)-3\\delta(t-4) \\\\\n&= 36\\delta(t-4)-3\\delta(t-4) \\\\\n&= 33\\delta(t-4)\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the simplified result is $33\\delta(t-4)$. The two terms can be merged because they share the same shift, and the function value is taken at that same impulse location before the coefficients are added.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 6,
        "lesson_title": "Multiplying a Function by a Shifted Impulse",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Multiplying a Piecewise Function by a Shifted Impulse",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Simplify $f(t)\\delta(t-2)$ for $f(t)=\\{t,\\, t<1;\\, 3-t,\\, t\\ge 1\\}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is $\\delta(t-2)$, so sample the piecewise function at $t=2$. Since $2\\ge 1$, use the branch $3-t$ and evaluate it at that impulse location.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\nf(t)\\delta(t-2) &= f(2)\\delta(t-2) \\\\\n&= (3-2)\\delta(t-2) \\\\\n&= 1\\delta(t-2)\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "So the simplified product is $1\\delta(t-2)$. The only extra step is choosing the correct branch before applying the shifted-impulse rule.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
